\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Cells of a gasket graph meet at vertices}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{For every #{n\ge 0}, the \vocabk{gasket graph}{fdip-00DP}
#{G_n} is connected, its #{n}-cells partition its edges, and
any two distinct #{n}-cells share at most one vertex.
Midpoints of edges from distinct #{n}-cells are distinct.}

\proof{
\p{The graph #{G_0} is one cell, and it is connected. That cell
contains every edge. Only one cell is present. Suppose the
claims hold for #{G_n}. Inside one parent cell the 9 edges run from #{A} to #{b},
from #{A} to #{c}, from #{b} to #{c}, from #{B} to #{a}, from
#{B} to #{c}, from #{a} to #{c}, from #{C} to #{a}, from
#{C} to #{b}, and from #{a} to #{b}. Each of the three
triangles uses three of these edges, and the 9 edges are
pairwise distinct.
Distinct parent cells use distinct midpoints, because the
#{n}-cells partition the edges of #{G_n}. Edges coming from
different parents are therefore distinct, and the
#{(n+1)}-cells partition the edges of #{G_{n+1}}. Each edge
of #{G_{n+1}} receives one midpoint, so midpoints of edges
from distinct #{(n+1)}-cells are distinct.}

\p{Two of the three triangles inside one parent have vertex
sets #{\{A,b,c\}} and #{\{B,a,c\}}, or a pair of the same
shape, so they share one midpoint. A triangle in another
parent uses other midpoints. Its only vertex in #{V_n} is
its corner. Distinct parents share at most one vertex, so
triangles from different parents share at most that vertex.}

\p{A path in #{G_n} becomes a path in #{G_{n+1}} when each
edge is replaced by the two edges through its midpoint. Every
midpoint lies on such a path. Thus #{G_{n+1}} is connected.}
}
